content.text:
The "Define your security config as code" example is now a check only your own tenant would write: the groups you use to assign licences must exist and have members.

```powershell
function Test-CT1001 {
    [MaesterTest(Id = 'CT.1001',
        Title = 'Our licensing groups are not empty')]
    param()
    # The groups that assign licences in this tenant
    foreach ($name in 'LIC-M365-E5', 'LIC-Copilot', 'LIC-Intune-Suite') {
        $group = Invoke-MtGraphRequest 'groups' -Filter "displayName eq '$name'"
        if (-not $group) { return $false }
        $members = Invoke-MtGraphRequest "groups/$($group.id)/members"
        if (-not $members) { return $false }
    }
    $true
}
```

- The test fails if one of the named groups is missing or has no members. That's the kind of regression that silently removes everyone's licences after a cleanup script or an AI agent tidies up groups.
- The calls use parameters that `Invoke-MtGraphRequest` really has, but I haven't run the function against a tenant.
- To make every line fit without wrapping on desktop, I gave the code block a little more of the width than the text column (about 55/45 instead of half and half).

I checked it on localhost:3200. The change is in [index.astro](src/pages/index.astro), and nothing is committed.

